<BlogPost
  title="Best Running Games That Actually Make You Run (2026)"
  description="The best running games and apps that turn exercise into play — from Zombies, Run! to territory games like Stride. Honest picks for runners who get bored easily."
  path="/blog/best-running-games"
  published="2026-06-22"
  tag="Games"
  readTime="7 min"
  faqs={faqs}
  related={{ href: '/blog/how-to-make-running-fun/', label: 'How to make running fun' }}
>
  <p>
    Willpower is overrated. If you struggle to run, the fix usually isn’t more discipline — it’s a better
    <em>reason</em>. That’s what a good running game gives you: a goal, visible progress, and stakes that pull
    you out the door. Here are the running games and apps actually worth your time in 2026, what each is best
    for, and who should pick which.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Stride — run to own your city</h2>
  <p>
    We build Stride, so take this with the appropriate pinch of salt — but it exists because nothing else
    scratched this itch. Stride is a GPS run tracker that’s secretly a <strong>territory game</strong>: every
    loop you run claims the real area you enclose on a live 3D map of your streets, painted your colour. Rivals
    can run over your turf and steal it, weekly leagues reset the leaderboard, and clubs fight live
    club-vs-club battles. <strong>Best for:</strong> competitive runners who want a reason to run <em>today</em>.
    Live now on iPhone — <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/conquer-your-city-stride/id6776885395" target="_blank" rel="noopener">download Stride free on the App Store</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Zombies, Run!</h2>
  <p>
    The original running game with a cult following. You’re a runner in a zombie apocalypse, collecting
    supplies and outrunning hordes through an audio story that plays over your music. <strong>Best for:</strong>
    solo runners who want immersion and don’t care about competition.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Run An Empire</h2>
  <p>
    A territory-claiming game in the same family as Stride — you capture areas of the real map and defend them
    over time, with a historical empire theme. <strong>Best for:</strong> players who like the land-grab idea
    with a slower, strategy-game pace.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The Conqueror Challenges</h2>
  <p>
    Sign up for a virtual challenge — run the distance of the Great Wall, Route 66, or a marathon — and your
    progress moves you along a real map, ending with a physical medal. <strong>Best for:</strong> goal-chasers
    who love a finish line and a reward in the post.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Pikmin Bloom</h2>
  <p>
    From the makers of Pokémon GO. A gentle, location-based walking game where every step grows plants and
    little helpers. It’s built for walkers more than runners, but the daily step loop is genuinely sticky.
    <strong>Best for:</strong> casual movers who want something low-pressure and charming.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Walkr / Wokamon</h2>
  <p>
    Idle games that turn your steps into fuel — power a galaxy spaceship or grow a creature. Light, fun, and
    forgiving. <strong>Best for:</strong> people who want their step count to <em>do</em> something without any
    competitive pressure.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Strava (segments & local legends)</h2>
  <p>
    Not a game by design, but its segments, KOM/QOM crowns and Local Legend status add a competitive layer
    plenty of runners get hooked on. <strong>Best for:</strong> fast runners who want to chase course records.
    If that loop has gone stale for you, that’s exactly the gap a
    <a href="/strava-alternative/">territory-based Strava alternative</a> fills.
  </p>

  <h2>How to pick</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Want a story?</strong> Zombies, Run!</li>
    <li><strong>Want a finish line + medal?</strong> The Conqueror.</li>
    <li><strong>Want to compete and conquer real turf?</strong> Stride or Run An Empire.</li>
    <li><strong>Want something gentle for steps?</strong> Pikmin Bloom or Walkr.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Whatever you choose, the principle is the same: the run you’ll actually do is the one that feels like play.
    Pick the game whose loop you can’t put down, and let it pull you out the door.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
